{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_b6amdul33nnqzkiy27ihmpycbvwmalgsebezfab42ofqcbrcajoq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both `cargo build` and `cargo test` pass successfully. The single test (`test_circular_buffer`) passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced every `unimplemented!()` in `src/circular_buffer.rs` with implementations matching the C source in `c_src/src/circular_buffer.c`:\n\n- **`new`** — already implemented (allocates buffer with `vec![0; size]`)\n- **`reset`** — clears head/tail offsets and `data_size` (Rust `usize` can't be `-1`, so I use `0` and rely on `data_size == 0` to disambiguate the \"empty\" state)\n- **`get_size` / `get_capacity` / `get_data_size`** — return their respective fields\n- **`free`** — explicitly drops `self`; Rust's drop semantics handle deallocation automatically\n- **`push`** — handles the same three cases as the C version: oversized writes are truncated to the last `size` bytes, writes that fit linearly without wrap, and writes that overflow and wrap around (advancing the head past overwritten data). I used modular arithmetic `% size` to express the wrap-around cleanly, since Rust's `usize` cannot be `-1`\n- **`inter_read`** — handles the three layouts (linear segment, wrap not needed, wrap needed across two segments). When `reset_head` is true, I advance the head, and when the buffer becomes empty, reset offsets back to `0`\n- **`pop`** — wraps `inter_read(.., true)`\n- **`read`** — non-mutating variant of the read logic (mirrors `inter_read(.., false)` but takes `&self`)\n- **`print`** — formats the buffer to stdout, using `_` for unused slots, matching the C version's hex/ascii formatting\n\n`cargo build` succeeds (warnings are only in `src/lib.rs` and `src/bin/`, which I was instructed not to modify), and `cargo test` reports `1 passed; 0 failed`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_i